Predicting cryptographic keys by exploiting a weak Random Number Generator (RNG) is one of the most devastating attacks an elite hacker can execute. When the randomness fails, the mathematical walls of security collapse entirely. [1, 2, 3] 
In software development, there are two types of random generators:

   1. PRNG (Pseudo-Random Number Generator): Fast, mathematical formulas (like the Mersenne Twister used in Python's standard random module or JavaScript's Math.random()). They look random but are completely deterministic. [4, 5, 6] 
   2. CSPRNG (Cryptographically Secure PRNG): Uses environmental noise (entropy) to ensure that even if you see a million outputs, you cannot mathematically calculate the next one. [5, 7] 

If a developer mistakenly uses a standard PRNG to generate cryptographic keys, tokens, or initialization vectors (IVs), an attacker can easily hijack the machine. [1, 8] 
Here is exactly how a hacker cracks a weak PRNG to predict cryptographic keys. [9] 
