## 🌍 Real-World Impact
This math vulnerability is not just theoretical; it has caused catastrophic security disasters:

* 
* The Debian OpenSSL Flaw (CVE-2008-0166): A developer accidentally removed a line of code responsible for gathering entropy. As a result, the PRNG only used the Process ID (PID) as its seed. Because there are only 32,768 possible PIDs, the total number of security keys that could ever be generated dropped from billions to a tiny handful. Hackers easily pre-computed every single possible key and gained root access to thousands of enterprise servers worldwide. [2, 7, 12] 
* The Dual_EC_DRBG Backdoor: A random number generator approved by NIST was discovered to have a mathematical "trapdoor". The constants were meticulously chosen so that anyone who knew a specific relationship between two points on an elliptic curve could predict all future outputs after observing just 32 bytes of data. [1, 2, 13, 14] 
